{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S6 — Bounded concurrent gate executions, with fast-fail admission\n\nImplements the decision headed **\"Bounded concurrency with FAST-FAIL admission (S6)\"** in the\nLANDED `docs/adr/0112-on-box-disk-ceiling-architecture.md` on `origin/main`. Decisions are cited\nby HEADING, never number: prerequisite 7dfa records an earlier, shorter Decision list whose\nnumbers do not match the merged file.\n\n## Why\n\nS2–S5 cap each accumulator with a high-water-mark GC, and **a watermark GC assumes evictable\ndata exists**. The janitor takes no per-reader lease and relies on POSIX delete-on-last-close,\nso it may `unlink` an in-flight snapshot but the blocks return only when the last reader closes.\nUnder the operator's **~10 parallel plan reviews** the held bytes are LIVE, not garbage: the GC\nfinds nothing evictable and the volume fills anyway. Both gates copy the repo at a ref and share\n**ONE counter** (two caps of N each admit 2N); at capacity the call **fast-fails**, since a\nqueued gate still holds its thread, ~739 MB resident, and the client's request.\n\n## Design\n\n**Seam.** `peak_rss.gate_peak_rss` already wraps this scope and says why: wrapping\n`review_plan()` / `verify_completion()` \"covers both call paths at one seam, since both reach\nthe gate through these functions\". Admission takes that seam but sits strictly OUTSIDE it, since\n`gate_peak_rss` wraps only the run while the bytes are spent earlier. It becomes the **outermost\n`with`** in each entry point, enclosing every byte-spending step: in `review_plan`,\n`resolve_gate_handle(...)` plus the run; in `verify_completion` it must open before\n`resolve_gate_handle` AND enclose `_resolve_completion_ticket_view` (which materializes the\npinned ticket store and may rebind `handle`) plus the `try/finally` closing that view — the\nwhole remainder of the function.\n\n**Counter.** N slot files in the store's existing `locks/` dir, each held for the run under a\nnon-blocking `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`, released in a `finally`. Cross-process (AC4) and\ncrash-safe (AC5: the kernel drops a flock when the holder dies — no heartbeat, which the janitor\nrejected as unsound). It also serialises across threads of one process, since a flock is held by\nthe open file description and each acquisition opens its own; and it inherits\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR`, following gate scratch onto the ADR's dedicated volume.\n\n**Degradation is SPLIT**, per the ADR's consequence that admission must treat an unreachable\nscratch volume as a refusal, not \"an empty cache to repopulate onto the root filesystem\":\n\n- **No `fcntl`, or one slot file unusable inside a healthy `locks/` dir** — ADMIT, and emit one\n  `GATE_ADMISSION_DISARMED {json}` marker naming the reason. Refusing would turn a platform gap\n  into a total gate outage, and the free-space floor still applies; but a silently disarmed cap\n  is how bug 3276 recurs, so it is never silent.\n- **The store root unreachable** (scratch volume unmounted or read-only, so `store_root()` /\n  `locks/` cannot be created) — **REFUSE**, with `GateScratchUnavailableError` (same `LLMError`\n  base and retryable outcome, its own `gate_scratch_unavailable` code). Admitting would silently\n  relocate gate scratch back onto the root filesystem — the failure this epic prevents. No\n  marker: a raised refusal already reaches the caller and its log.\n\n**Refusal — the hierarchy decides the mapping.** `GateCongestedError` lives in\n`src/rebar/llm/errors.py` as a **direct subclass of `LLMError`** (not `LLMRunnerError`: no\nrunner ran) and attaches `.outcome = LLMOutcome(WAIT_AND_RETRY, retryable=True)` in `__init__`.\nThose two facts fire the mapping through arms that ALREADY exist, so **no new `except` clause is\nadded**:\n\n- MCP: `_review_plan_body` / `_verify_completion_body` catch `rebar.llm.LLMError` →\n  `_structured_llm_failure`, which reads `outcome_of(exc)` (`getattr(exc, \"outcome\", None)`), so\n  the attached outcome gives `retryable: true` (without it, `false`), and no `verdict` key is\n  emitted — congestion is unreadable as INDETERMINATE, BLOCK or FAIL.\n- CLI: both handlers catch `llm.LLMError` → `_llm_error_exit_code`, returning **11** (\"transient\n  — retry\") exactly when `outcome.retryable`, else 1, after an `Error:` line.\n- `_errors._llm_error_code` gains branches for both new types returning `\"gate_congested\"` /\n  `\"gate_scratch_unavailable\"`, in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`, placed **before every existing arm** (the\n  bare `isinstance(exc, LLMError)` would otherwise report them as `command_failed`). The\n  error-taxonomy contract test asserts the family is EXHAUSTIVELY mapped, so both are registered\n  there too — it verifies this in-session.\n\n**Metric (AC7).** One line-start `GATE_CONGESTED {json}` stderr marker per refusal, following\nthe `GATE_PEAK_RSS` journald convention (the containers hold no AWS credentials) — also the\nportable choice: identical under the CLI with no CloudWatch and no CI provider.\n\n**Config (AC6).** `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, resolved by `resolve_gate_max_concurrent`\nbeside `resolve_gate_min_free_bytes`. `0` disables the bound (the `max_bytes` idiom); a\nmalformed or negative value falls back to the default rather than disarming the cap. No\n`REBAR_GATE_*` env tier, deviating from the ADR's measured-defaults decision: a new `REBAR_*`\nliteral is a new `env_var` mechanism, unlandable today per bug `a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a`, while\n`[snapshot]` keys are exempt (`_RESERVED_SECTIONS`, absent from `_SECTIONS`).\n\n**Default = 4, from measurement.** `GATE_PEAK_RSS`: plan_review ~748 MB, verify_completion\n~462 MB (the ADR cites ~739 MB and a degraded run at 501 MB, so it varies). The host is a\n`t4g.large` (8 GiB) shared with Gerrit and the ADR's host-sizing decision forbids growing it;\n~2.17 GB steady state plus Gerrit's ~3 GiB reservation leave roughly 3 GiB for gate work.\n4 × 748 MB ≈ 2.99 GB fits; a fifth run (~3.74 GB) does not — and 4 is well below the observed\n~10 concurrent, so the cap actually sheds load.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: one counter bounds both gates — hold `limit` slots as `plan_review`, assert a\n  `verify_completion` admission is refused.\n- [ ] AC2: the refused call returns in under one second while a holder still runs (it\n  fast-fails rather than blocking or queueing).\n- [ ] AC3a: the message names host congestion and says to retry; on the review-bot path this is\n  ADR 0069's retryable deferral, never an `LLM-Review −1`.\n- [ ] AC3b: not readable as a verdict — the MCP body returns `retryable: true`,\n  `error == \"gate_congested\"`, and NO `verdict` key.\n- [ ] AC3c: the CLI returns exit 11 for a congestion refusal.\n- [ ] AC4: a real child process holds every slot and this process is refused.\n- [ ] AC5a: SIGKILL a holder, assert a later admission wins (no lost slot).\n- [ ] AC5b: raise inside the wrapper, assert the next admission succeeds.\n- [ ] AC6a: the cap reads `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, defaults to 4, treats 0 as off, and\n  falls back to the default on a malformed or negative value — one test each.\n- [ ] AC6b: `docs/config.md` and `docs/plan-review-gate.md` both name the key, default and\n  rationale; the latter says a congestion refusal is an expected outcome, not a failure.\n- [ ] AC7: a refusal emits exactly one `GATE_CONGESTED` marker naming gate, ticket and limit; an\n  admitted gate emits none.\n- [ ] AC8a: with `fcntl` unavailable the gate is admitted AND one `GATE_ADMISSION_DISARMED`\n  marker naming the reason is emitted.\n- [ ] AC8b: with `store_root()` raising, the gate is REFUSED, not admitted.\n\n## Testing & file impact\n\n`pytest tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py -q`, the taxonomy contract test, `make lint`,\n`make typecheck`, `pytest tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py -q`.\n\nNew: `llm/gate_admission.py`, `tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py`. Edited: `llm/errors.py`,\n`llm/plan_review/__init__.py`, `llm/completion.py`, `_config_resolvers.py`, `_errors.py`, the\ntaxonomy contract test, `docs/config.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`. Unchanged by design:\n`_mcp_llm.py`, `_cli/_llm_commands.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788495397954287001,"uuid":"51dc5fee-1e81-4535-8b82-603277c702d5"}